+# What a workout is
+
+> **DRAFT, written by an agent for the user to edit.** TRAINER.md: *a human writes the methodology.* Nothing here is the user's until they have edited it, and nothing reads it yet.
+
+The planner's criteria for a good workout. The engine still decides only what is possible (ADR-0001); this document informs the choices the planner charter leaves free, and dictates none of them. Every number is a default: departing from one is allowed, and the block's `reason` says why.
+
+**Goal assumed:** muscle and general strength, for one person training alone with little equipment. *(User: confirm or replace. Onboarding picks the goal from session 13.)*
+
+## 1. The time buys sets, not exercises
+
+| when | do | not |
+|---|---|---|
+| time is left over | add a set to an exercise already in the plan | add another exercise |
+| time is short | drop an exercise, keep the sets of the rest | cut every exercise to one set |
+| a block is cheap | ask whether it is cheap because it is a circuit | add exercises to it until it fits |
+
+One exercise done for three or four hard sets trains a muscle more than four exercises done for one set each.
+
+## 2. How many exercises a group gets
+
+| group's share of the workout | exercises |
+|---|---|
+| under ~10 min | 1 |
+| ~10-20 min | 2 |
+| over ~20 min | 2-3 |
+
+- **The second exercise must differ from the first** in pattern, angle or muscle: a press and a fly, a horizontal and a vertical pull, a triceps and a biceps exercise. Two variants of the same movement (four push-ups) count as one exercise done four times.
+- **For `arms`, `legs` and `core`** the group spans several muscles (`muscle-groups.md`), so its exercises go to different muscles before any muscle gets a second.
+- A compound first, then isolation if there is time.
+
+## 3. What a working set is
+
+- **Close to failure**: the set ends with about 0-3 reps left in reserve. A set that ends far from failure is a warm-up, not a working set.
+- **Reps**: 6-15 for most work, up to 20-30 for isolation and body weight. Heavy work under 6 reps only where the load allows it.
+- **Body weight and bands**: the load cannot be raised, so the *exercise* is chosen to land the reps in range. If the user can do 30, pick the harder variant (`variant_of` / `regression_of` in the catalog), not more reps.
+- **Timed work**: 20-60 s holds; a carry or a plank is a working set when the last seconds are hard.
+- **Sets per exercise**: 2-4 working sets (rounds). Over the cycle, about 10-20 working sets per muscle is the reference.
+
+## 4. Which rest goes with which intent
+
+| intent | rest after a round |
+|---|---|
+| heavy compound, under 6 reps | 2.5-3 min |
+| compound, 6-15 reps | 1.5-2.5 min |
+| isolation | 60-90 s |
+| superset of two non-competing muscles | 1.5-2 min after the pair |
+| conditioning circuit | 30-60 s |
+
+A short rest before a hard compound set costs reps on the next set. Shortening rest to fit the clock is a cost to say in the `reason`, not a free saving.
+
+## 5. Straight sets, superset or circuit
+
+| structure | right when | the cheap version |
+|---|---|---|
+| **straight sets** | the default for compounds and for the main exercise of the day | - |
+| **superset** | the two exercises do not compete: push with pull, biceps with triceps, upper with lower, a main lift with core | two exercises for the same muscle, so the second is done tired |
+| **circuit** | the intent is conditioning, or every station is a different group and the time is genuinely short | several exercises for one muscle, chosen because three rests cost less than fifteen |
+
+A superset of non-competing muscles saves time without costing reps; that is the honest reason to use one. A circuit of variants of one movement is the shape that fits the clock, not a method.
+
+## Worked example: the plan that opened ticket 11.2
+
+Chest and arms, 45 minutes, body weight and bands.
+
+| what it did | what this document asks |
+|---|---|
+| four push-up variants in one circuit, 8 reps | one push-up variant hard enough that 8-15 reps is near failure, 3-4 straight sets, then one band fly or crossover |
+| five triceps exercises in one circuit | one triceps and one biceps exercise, supersetted because they do not compete |
+| 25 of 45 minutes | the leftover time goes into sets (§1) |
+
+## Sources
+
+*(Agent-listed from memory. User: verify each before it stays.)*
+
+| claim | source |
+|---|---|
+| more weekly sets per muscle, more growth; ~10+ as a reference | Schoenfeld, Ogborn, Krieger (2017), *J Sports Sci* 35(11) |
+| a wide rep range grows muscle when sets are near failure; heavy loads for maximal strength | Schoenfeld, Grgic, Ogborn, Krieger (2017), *J Strength Cond Res* 31(12) |
+| proximity to failure and hypertrophy | Refalo et al. (2023), *Sports Med* 53 |
+| longer rest between sets favours strength and growth | Schoenfeld et al. (2016), *J Strength Cond Res* 30(7); Grgic et al. (2018), *Sports Med* 48 |
+| supersets and time-efficient programme design | Weakley et al. (2017), *Eur J Appl Physiol* 117; Iversen et al. (2021), *Sports Med* 51 |
+| order, rest and progression for healthy adults | ACSM position stand (2009), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 41(3) |
